        <div id="paint-stain-sealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Paint, Stain or Sealer: Decide This First
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Paint</strong> forms a colored film on top of the surface. It covers stains, patches and mismatched concrete, and it offers the most color control. It also needs the most preparation and can peel if prep or moisture conditions are wrong. On a driveway, only paints rated for vehicle traffic and exterior use are worth considering.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Concrete stain</strong> soaks into the concrete or bonds as a thin, translucent or solid layer. Penetrating stains do not peel, though they fade over time and do not hide stains or patches as well as paint. Solid-color stains sit between paint and translucent stain in appearance and durability. If your concrete is in good shape and you mainly want to change or refresh color, stain is often a safer choice than paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clear sealer</strong> protects without changing color much, or adds a wet look depending on the product. For concrete that just needs protection from water, salt and oil, a penetrating sealer is the lowest-maintenance option. For asphalt, a black sealer is the right refresh product. Our guides to the <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best concrete driveway sealers</a> and <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> cover those routes.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
