                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A practical rule: if the concrete is sound and evenly colored, consider stain or sealer before paint. If it is stained, patched or mismatched and you want a uniform look, paint makes sense, as long as you are prepared to do the prep. If it is asphalt, choose a black sealer or filler-sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Concrete and Asphalt Need Different Products
          </h2>

          <p>
            All of the colored paints on this list are for concrete. Concrete is rigid, porous and alkaline, and epoxy-modified acrylic paints are built to bond to it once it is cleaned and lightly etched. They handle the abrasion and heat of tires better than standard porch paints, though not as well as professionally installed two-part epoxy or polyaspartic systems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Asphalt is a flexible, petroleum-based pavement. Standard concrete and porch paints are not designed for it: they can crack as the pavement flexes, and oils in the asphalt can soften or stain them. The right way to refresh asphalt is an asphalt sealer or filler-sealer, which restores a uniform black and protects the binder. Decorative colored coatings for asphalt exist in the commercial world, but they are specialty products mostly applied by contractors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garage floor epoxy kits deserve a mention because many people consider them for driveways. Many two-part garage epoxies are designed for interior use and can yellow or chalk in direct sunlight, and some labels specifically exclude exterior use. Check the label before using any garage product outdoors. For interior slabs, see our <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a> rankings.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Paint is for concrete. Asphalt gets sealer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your driveway is blacktop, the product you want is a black asphalt sealer or filler-sealer, not paint. If it is concrete, choose a paint rated for exterior use and vehicle traffic.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What to Look For in a Driveway Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Exterior rating.</strong> The label should explicitly list exterior concrete. Sun, rain and freeze-thaw cycles are harder on coatings than an indoor garage, and products formulated only for interiors may fade, chalk or peel outside.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hot-tire pickup resistance.</strong> Warm tires that have just come off the road can soften paint and lift it when a car parks and later pulls away. Products marketed for garage floors and vehicle traffic typically address this. Basic porch paints often do not, which is why they tend to fail first in the tire paths.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Slip resistance.</strong> A painted surface is smoother than bare broom-finished concrete and can be slippery when wet, especially on slopes. Look for products that accept an anti-slip additive, or buy a separate additive designed for floor paint. <strong>Coverage and coats.</strong> Labels typically list coverage per gallon on smooth concrete; porous or rough driveways absorb more. Plan for two coats on most driveways.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Color.</strong> Medium grays and tans hide tire marks, dirt and leaves better than very light or very dark colors. Very dark colors absorb more heat, which can make hot-tire pickup worse in sunny climates. If you want a design, plan borders and accents in a porch paint and use a vehicle-rated paint in the driving areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Recoat and repair path.</strong> Before buying, read how the manufacturer says to touch up or recoat the product in two or three years. One-part acrylic-based paints can usually be scuffed, cleaned and recoated with the same product. Some harder coatings need aggressive sanding before a new coat will bond. A product that is easy to maintain often beats a tougher one that is a chore to refresh, because tire paths will need attention eventually no matter what you choose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Prepping Concrete So Paint Sticks
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>1. Check for moisture.</strong> Tape a square of clear plastic, about 18 inches on a side, to the driveway with all edges sealed, and leave it for 24 hours in the sun. If condensation forms under the plastic or the concrete underneath is darker, moisture is coming up from below, and a film-forming paint is likely to blister or peel. That driveway is a candidate for a penetrating stain or sealer instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>2. Clean and degrease.</strong> Sweep, then scrub with a concrete cleaner or degreaser, paying special attention to oil and fluid spots. Paint will not bond to oil. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a> covers the tougher spots. A pressure washer helps rinse residue thoroughly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>3. Etch or profile.</strong> Most floor paints require a slightly rough surface. Use a concrete etcher per the paint manufacturer's instructions, or grind the surface for best results. A properly etched surface feels like medium-grit sandpaper. If water beads on the surface after cleaning, there is likely an old sealer that must be removed before painting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>4. Repair and fill.</strong> Fill cracks with a flexible concrete sealant and patch chips and spalls with a patching compound. Our list of the <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">best driveway crack fillers</a> covers both. <strong>5. Let it dry fully.</strong> After washing and etching, allow at least a day or two of dry weather before painting, or longer per the label. New concrete typically needs about 28 days of curing before any coating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Applying Driveway Paint for a Finish That Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pick a stretch of dry weather in the temperature range on the label, commonly somewhere around 50 to 90°F for both air and surface, with no rain for at least a day. Avoid painting in direct midday sun on very hot days, which can cause the paint to dry too quickly and show lap marks. Mornings and late afternoons often work best.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut in edges with a brush first, then roll the main area with a 3/8- to 1/2-inch nap roller on an extension pole. Work in manageable sections, keeping a wet edge to avoid lap lines. Apply thin, even coats rather than one heavy coat. Thick coats take longer to cure and are more prone to peeling and hot-tire pickup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wait the recoat time on the label before applying a second coat. If you are using an anti-slip additive, many manufacturers recommend mixing it into or broadcasting it onto the final coat. Stir often so the grit stays suspended.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Label Guidance, 1-Part Epoxy</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Coats on most driveways</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50-90°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common application range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Foot traffic, varies by label</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~7 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Vehicle traffic, varies by label</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Respect the vehicle traffic time. Many one-part epoxy paints list several days to about a week before parking, and parking early is a leading cause of hot-tire pickup and peeling. Keep cars off longer in cool or humid weather. For more on concrete floor coatings generally, our <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/">best concrete floor paint</a> guide compares products for other surfaces.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="color" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing a Color That Still Looks Good in Year Three
          </h2>

          <p>
            Color choice affects how long a painted driveway looks fresh far more than most buyers expect. Tires leave dark rubber marks, leaves and pollen leave tan and yellow stains, and road grit settles into every low spot. A mid-tone gray, slate or warm tan hides all three. Pure white and very light gray show every mark within weeks, while near-black shows dust, salt residue and dried water spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat is the other reason to avoid very dark shades on concrete. Dark coatings absorb more sun, which raises surface temperatures on summer afternoons and makes softening under warm tires more likely. In the Sun Belt and on south-facing drives, a medium color is the safer choice for durability as well as appearance. Lighter colors also keep the slab cooler underfoot near pools and patios.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the house, not the swatch card. Paint a two-foot test square in an out-of-the-way corner, let it cure for a few days, and look at it in morning and late-afternoon light before committing. Colors look noticeably darker and more saturated on a large horizontal surface than on a small chip, so many owners go one shade lighter than their first pick.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a design, keep it simple and put it where tires do not run. A contrasting border along the edges, a band where the driveway meets the sidewalk, or a decorative apron near the garage door can look sharp in a porch paint, while the driving lanes stay in a single vehicle-rated color. For a stone or tile look on concrete without paint, our guide to <a href="/how-to-stain-concrete-patio-to-look-like-stone/">staining concrete to look like stone</a> covers a stain-based approach that will not peel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="asphalt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Refreshing and Coloring Asphalt
          </h2>

          <p>
            For asphalt, the standard refresh is a black sealer or filler-sealer applied with a squeegee. It covers faded gray asphalt, hides minor patches and gives a uniform finish. Fill cracks first, clean the surface thoroughly, and follow the same weather rules as any water-based sealer. Our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer rankings</a> cover the main options in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some homeowners want a color other than black on asphalt, for example red or green for a decorative look. Colored asphalt coatings exist, mostly acrylic products used on tennis courts, playgrounds and commercial walkways. They tend to require careful prep and are often applied by contractors. Ordinary concrete or porch paints are not a good substitute.
          </p>

          <p>
            Parking lines and stencils on asphalt call for traffic or striping paint, which is formulated for pavement and dries quickly. Use it sparingly on a home driveway, and remember it can be slippery when wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your asphalt has significant cracking, raveling or potholes, no coating will make it look new for long. Address the structural issues first, and consider whether resurfacing makes more sense than coating. Our pages on <a href="/sealing-asphalt-driveway-pros-and-cons/">the pros and cons of sealing asphalt</a> and <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a> walk through those choices.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="traction" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Slip Resistance and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Painted concrete is noticeably smoother than bare concrete, and wet paint, ice, leaves or a film of oil on top make it slicker still. On a sloped driveway, near steps or where people walk to the door, slip resistance should be a priority, not an afterthought.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anti-slip additives are fine grit, often polymer beads or silica, that you mix into the final coat or broadcast onto it while wet. Many paint brands sell their own. They add a slight texture that improves grip without making the surface rough on bare feet. Follow the paint manufacturer's recommendation on type and amount.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use less gloss on driveways. Low-luster or satin finishes tend to be less slippery than high gloss, and they hide dirt and tire marks better. If you are painting near a pool, entry or steps, consider a product designed specifically for slip resistance in wet areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilation and fumes matter less outdoors, but follow the label's safety directions anyway. Wear gloves and eye protection when etching, since concrete etchers are often acidic, and keep children and pets off until the paint has cured.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-life" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Cost, Coverage and How Long It Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, one-part epoxy driveway paints typically run roughly $40 to $70 per gallon, with coverage often listed in the range of a few hundred square feet per gallon per coat on smooth concrete. A two-car driveway of around 600 square feet usually needs several gallons for two coats, plus cleaner, degreaser, etcher, rollers and possibly anti-slip additive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Porch and patio paints usually cost a bit less per gallon. Asphalt sealers and filler-sealers are sold in five-gallon pails, with prices that vary widely between budget and premium products. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">driveway sealing costs</a> has more detail on the asphalt side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lifespan depends heavily on prep, climate and traffic. Owners commonly report that a well-prepared one-part epoxy paint on concrete lasts a few years before needing touch-ups in tire paths, while poorly prepared surfaces can peel within a season. Asphalt sealers typically need recoating every two to three years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan for maintenance from the start. Keep a quart of leftover paint for touch-ups, clean oil drips promptly, and inspect the tire paths each spring. Touching up a small worn area is easy; repainting an entire peeling driveway means stripping first, which is a much bigger job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be honest about whether paint is worth it for your driveway. If the slab is badly cracked, heaving or spalling across large areas, paint will highlight the damage rather than hide it, and the money is better spent on repairs or replacement. If the concrete is sound and just stained or dull, a weekend of careful prep and two coats is one of the cheapest ways to change how the front of a house looks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting over moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete that wicks moisture from below pushes paint off as blisters and peeling, no matter how good the product. Tape down a square of plastic for 24 hours first. If condensation forms underneath, choose a penetrating stain or sealer instead of a film-forming paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the etch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Floor paint needs a slightly rough surface to grip. Smooth or sealed concrete lets paint sit on top and peel under tires. Etch or grind per the paint manufacturer's directions, rinse thoroughly, and make sure water soaks in rather than beading before you paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using porch paint in the tire paths</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Porch and patio paints are built for foot traffic. Under car tires, especially warm ones, they scuff, lift and show hot-tire marks quickly. Use a paint rated for vehicle traffic in driving and parking areas, and keep porch paint for walks and borders.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting asphalt with concrete paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete and porch paints are rigid and not designed for flexible, petroleum-based asphalt. They crack and can be softened by oils in the pavement. Refresh blacktop with an asphalt sealer or filler-sealer, and leave colored asphalt coatings to specialty products.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many paints feel dry within hours but need several days to a week to cure before vehicle traffic. Parking early is a leading cause of hot-tire pickup and peeling. Follow the label's vehicle traffic time and add extra days in cool or humid weather.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will paint hold up on a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with a paint rated for exterior concrete and vehicle traffic, such as a one-part epoxy-acrylic paint. Success depends on prep: the concrete must be dry, clean, degreased and etched or ground. Check for moisture with a plastic sheet test first. If moisture is present, a penetrating stain or sealer is usually a better choice than paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint an asphalt driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not with regular paint. Asphalt flexes and contains oils that can crack or soften concrete and porch paints. To refresh an asphalt driveway, use a black asphalt sealer or filler-sealer. Colored asphalt coatings exist but are specialty acrylic products, mostly used on courts and commercial surfaces and often applied by contractors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does driveway paint last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on prep, product, climate and traffic. A well-prepared one-part epoxy paint on concrete often lasts a few years before tire paths need touch-ups. Porch paint in tire areas wears faster. Poorly prepared surfaces can peel within a season. Asphalt sealers typically last two to three years before recoating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is hot-tire pickup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hot-tire pickup happens when warm tires that have just been driven park on a painted or coated surface, soften it, and then lift it as they pull away. It is a common failure in garages and driveways. Products marketed for vehicle traffic are formulated to resist it, and allowing the full cure time before parking helps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a primer for driveway paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many one-part epoxy floor paints are self-priming on properly etched bare concrete, but some manufacturers recommend a primer for very porous, previously painted or problem surfaces. Check the specific label. If repainting over old coatings, the old paint must be sound, cleaned and scuffed, and compatibility should be confirmed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is driveway paint slippery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Painted concrete is smoother than bare concrete and can be slippery when wet, especially on slopes. Choose a low-luster finish and add an anti-slip additive to the final coat, either mixed in or broadcast onto the wet paint. Pay extra attention near steps, entries and anywhere people walk regularly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is staining better than painting a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For sound, evenly colored concrete without moisture problems, staining is often lower maintenance because penetrating stains do not peel. Paint gives more color control and hides stains and patches better but requires more prep and maintenance. If moisture rises through the slab, stain or sealer is the safer option.
              </p>
