          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a concrete driveway, a one-part epoxy-acrylic paint such as Behr's or KILZ's, rated for exterior use and vehicle traffic, is the practical choice, with Seal-Krete Epoxy-Seal for a lower-odor option. For asphalt, skip paint entirely and use a black filler-sealer like Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield Blacktop or a premium sealer like Black Jack Drive-Maxx 1000.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The product matters less than the prep. Test for moisture, degrease, etch, fill cracks, apply two thin coats in good weather, and keep cars off until the label says otherwise. Do those things and a painted driveway can look good for years; skip them and it will not.
